zEwOTg=","url":"https://api.github.com/repos/angular/angular/labels/area:%20common/http","name":"area: common/http","color":"c5def5","default":false,"description":"Issues related to HTTP and HTTP Client"}],"state":"closed","locked":true,"assignees":[],"milestone":null,"comments":3,"created_at":"2017-10-20T12:14:40Z","updated_at":"2019-09-12T22:51:27Z","closed_at":"2017-11-29T18:12:25Z","assignee":null,"author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","type":null,"active_lock_reason":null,"sub_issues_summary":{"total":0,"completed":0,"percent_completed":0},"issue_dependencies_summary":{"blocked_by":0,"total_blocked_by":0,"blocking":0,"total_blocking":0},"body":"## I'm submitting a...\r\n\r\n<pre><code>[X] Bug report</code></pre>\r\n\r\n## Current behavior\r\n\r\n```ts\r\nhttp.get<boolean>(`/api/canYouSeeMe`)\r\n  .subscribe(x => console.log(x));\r\n```\r\n\r\nIn such case if backend returns `false` or `0` straight in the body (without any nested object) the console print outs `null`. While `true` or `1` keeps what it is.\r\n\r\nYes. I know that it's strongly recommended for json APIs to return pure values wrapped into some object, but still.. it's not forbidden and seems like javascript supports parsing such values.\r\n\r\nAnd that's happening because of this line:\r\n https://github.com/angular/angular/blob/230b98d4ddffee4df25f62ff3c9ac39a2329abf6/packages/common/http/src/response.ts#L265\r\n\r\nObviously:\r\n```ts\r\nthis.body = true || null; // true\r\nthis.body = false || null; // null\r\nthis.body = 0 || null; // null\r\n```\r\n\r\n## Expected behavior\r\n\r\nKeep the **falsy** (`false`, `0`) value unchanged.\r\n\r\n## Current workaround\r\n\r\nSet the `responseType` to `text` and parse it for yourself.\r\n\r\n## Environment\r\n\r\n<pre><code>Angular versions: 4.x, 5.0.0-rc.3</code></pre>\r\n","closed_by":{"login":"mhevery","id":111951,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjExMTk1MQ==","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/111951?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.c